SM(537,{"n":"1 Corinthians 7-8","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 7-8.mp3","d":2092.4,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.88,"So verse 20, let each one remain in the same calling in which he was called."],[24.96,"Were you called while a slave?"],[26.64,"Do not be concerned about it."],[28.56,"But if you could be made free, rather use it."],[31.76,"For he who is called in the Lord while a slave is the Lord's freedman."],[35.52,"Likewise, he who is called while free is Christ's slave."],[39.04,"You are bought at a price, do not become slaves of men."],[42.24,"Rather, and let each one remain with God in that state in which he was called."],[45.84,"Okay, so he uses another example"],[48.16,"He's still within the realm of talking about marriage."],[50.98,"But he's giving these other examples, these other related examples."],[54.5,"And he's saying, look, if if you come to Christ and you're a slave, fine."],[59.24,"You're free in the sight of Christ."],[62.76,"Don't worry about trying to get free of your master."],[66.45,"Or demanding, you know, because from the very beginning, Christianity and the idea of slavery are not compatible."],[75.65,"But Christianity and disobedience or revolution are also incompatible."],[82.13,"So that's why St."],[83.09,"Paul has to say to a slave who becomes a Christian, look, you need to remain in the state where you are."],[89.65,"Don't run away."],[91.31,"Don't don't disobey the civil commands."],[95.57,"Because they would look at it, they would realize it was wrong for a human being to own another human being."],[99.97,"St. Paul says as much there when he says, You were bought with a price, meaning by Christ."],[102.93,"St So don't become slaves of men."],[107.13,"You shouldn't be owning each other."],[108.81,"Gregory of Nyssa famously, we have multiple years Paschal orations."],[115.05,"And every year he was telling all of his congregants that they needed to free all their slaves if they were going to be Christians."],[121.93,"Because slavery had not been outlawed yet in the Roman Empire in the fourth century."],[125.53,"It would be later by Christians."],[129.53,"But at that point, it was not illegal yet."],[132.17,"And so it was just him telling people: if you're a Christian, you cannot own another human being."],[138.73,"How ridiculous it is if that other human being now is also a Christian."],[144.17,"And you claim you own them, they're your brother."],[147.05,"This is absurd"],[149.71,"And so he's saying, and those of you, by the way, who are free."],[154.21,"You're Christ's slave."],[155.41,"He bought you."],[156.86,"He owns you."],[158.06,"So he's saying, in whatever state you became a Christian, be content."],[162.38,"Stay in that state."],[163.82,"Keep God's commandments."],[165.26,"Use the gifts He's given you."],[166.62,"Walk in the path He's put in front of you."],[169.02,"That Christianity does not mean you need to walk some different path."],[173.12,"You don't need to leave your spouse and become a celibate."],[176.72,"You don't need to go and find a spouse."],[179.54,"If you don't have one."],[181.06,"You don't have to become Jewish."],[184.04,"You don't have to stop being Jewish."],[185.96,"You don't have to become free if you're a slave."],[189.0,"You don't have to become a slave if you're free."],[191.18,"That's not what Christian life requires."],[194.7,"So verse 25."],[195.9,"Now concerning virgins, I have no commandment from the Lord, yet I give judgment as one whom the Lord in his mercy has made trustworthy."],[204.46,"So this is the second time he said this."],[206.39,"St. Paul here, he's dealing with pastoral matters."],[209.37,"So he says he has no commandment from the Lord."],[211.53,"It means, look, I don't have something I can pull out from the Hebrew scriptures, or something I know that Christ said that directly speaks answers the question."],[222.69,"rem Remberemember, he's answering their questions, that directly answers this question you asked me."],[227.23,"He said, I'm being honest."],[228.35,"So, but I'm someone who, as he says, whom the Lord His mercy has made trustworthy."],[234.27,"But but Christ chose me and put me in this position, so I'm telling you my best judgment, of how to handle this situation."],[243.56,"I suppose, therefore, that this is good because of the present distress, that it is good for a man to remain as he is."],[250.28,"Are you bound to a wife, do not seek to be loosed?"],[253.08,"Are you loose from a wife, do not seek a wife?"],[255.96,"But even if you do marry, you have not sinned."],[259.0,"And if a virgin marries, she has not sinned."],[261.72,"Nevertheless, such will have trouble in the flesh, but I would spare you."],[266.84,"So he says, with that the the present distress, with everything going on there, with all the problems, some of which we've already heard about, some more of which we're going to hear about in the future."],[275.82,"He says, My best judgment it's probably best to again just stay however you are right if you're not married great if you are married great but then he goes on to say but you know what if you're not married and you do get married that's not a sin right now notice he doesn't say the opposite he doesn't say you're okay to get divorced he doesn't say that but he does say hey but if you're but if you are right now single and you get married okay right that's not a problem either."],[309.84,"He's saying that you you may have some you may have some troubles, you may have some issues, but there's nothing sinful or or wrong about it."],[318.28,"But this I say, brethren, the time is short, so that from now on even, those who have wives should be as though they had none."],[321.88,"those who Those who weep as though they did not weep, rejoice as though they did not rejoice, those who buy as though they did not possess, and those who use this world as not misusing it."],[337.04,"For the form of this world is passing away."],[339.92,"So what is he talking about here?"],[342.0,"So a lot of people will sort of overinterpret this and they'll say, Oh, the time is short."],[345.6,"Well, this is St."],[346.32,"Paul thinking Jesus is gonna return like right now."],[349.94,"Now, of course, St."],[350.74,"Paul thought Christ could return right then, just like he could return right now."],[356.58,"But that's not what he's basing this on."],[358.58,"What he's what St."],[359.54,"Paul is trying to say is that these things he's talking about, and the more things he adds in this list, that we are all, he's saying, very involved in this world and what's going on."],[366.58,"we And this is what're focused on, and this is what we're thinking about."],[375.58,"If you're single, you're thinking about finding a wife or finding a husband."],[379.68,"Or you're thinking about this or that, or using the things that you're thinking about wealth and your career and your life, and right, he's saying all these things about your path and your water."],[389.44,"That's all stuff in this world."],[391.05,"So now St."],[391.69,"Paul is saying, look, this isn't the whole picture, this isn't the most important thing."],[396.41,"Okay, this isn't the most important thing."],[398.89,"Because either, either we're going to die, or Christ is going to return."],[405.5,"These things are coming."],[407.04,"And so we can't have sort of all of our focus on these issues here in the world."],[410.32,"All these things he's just listed"],[411.72,"And are we a slave?"],[412.76,"Are we not right?"],[414.92,"What do we eat?"],[415.72,"What are we eating and not eating?"],[417.9,"These are not the ultimate things."],[420.46,"These are not the ultimate things."],[422.22,"So he's saying these can't be your focus."],[425.74,"And so we need to always keep in mind, even as we're making these decisions and doing these things, we need to keep in mind the fact that, that this is not the ultimate, this is not the end, this is not the purpose, of our life."],[438.97,"And this is why he talks about mourning and rejoicing."],[441.29,"We mourn because we have some misfortune in this life right now, or we're rejoicing and we're all excited and happy because of some little thing that's happened."],[450.41,"He's saying, look, we need to detach a little bit."],[454.03,"From the from all the things going on in this world."],[456.43,"That this is part of what's causing the problems there in Corinth."],[459.39,"So you're all very involved in life in this world and the city and all the things that are happening, and they're sort of pulling your attention sort of away from Christ, and they're your focus."],[471.95,"Remember, he's looking at a list of questions from them, some of which we've just seen him addressing about marriage and slavery and this and that."],[480.65,"He's saying you know, I'm looking at this list of all your concerns, and these are not sort of ultimate concerns."],[490.01,"These are sort of your day-to-day concerns, which are important but not of ultimate."],[495.47,"Of ultimate importance."],[497.31,"But I want you. to be without care"],[499.75,"He who is unmarried cares for the things of the Lord, how he may please the Lord, but he who is married cares about the things of the world, how he may please his wife."],[509.59,"There's a difference between a wife and a virgin."],[511.91,"The unmarried woman cares about the things of the Lord, that she may be holy both in body and in spirit."],[516.87,"But she who is married cares about the things of the world, how she may please her husband."],[520.87,"And this I say for your own profit, not that I may put a leash on you, but for what is proper and that you may serve the Lord without distraction."],[529.43,"He's saying the reason why he's now a couple of times talked about celibacy or about, hey, if you're not married, it's good to stay that way."],[540.57,"He's being more specific as to why."],[543.17,"The reason why that is good is that if you're unmarried, if you're living a celibate life that allows you to be sort of more detached from these things of the world and of everyday life, it sort of facilitates that so that you can focus purely on these things of ultimate concern."],[561.82,"So this is basically the foundation here of monasticism."],[566.22,"A person can by becoming by living a celibate life, by not owning property, by not being concerned with the things of this world, sort of dedicate themselves entirely to that one concern."],[578.04,"Whereas he's saying, if you're married, you're always going to have, and it's right to have."],[584.3,"If you're if you're a husband and and you you have children're not at all concerned about providing for their needs, there's a problem."],[592.47,"That's not good."],[594.87,"So it is then going to be good for you to be taking care of those things, but that means you're always going to have sort of multiple multiple goals in mind."],[604.47,"And there's going to be a temptation for you, there's going to be more of a temptation for you to become too focused on the things of this world."],[613.43,"And so if you are married, that's what he means by those who are married to be like one who is unmarried."],[619.75,"He's saying if you're married, you need to remain focused as you can, as much as you can, on those things of ultimate concern, not give in to the temptation of just becoming totally. focused on paying the bills and getting new shoes because the kids outgrew them for the 18th time"],[636.69,"And all those things."],[638.14,"Where that just becomes all you think about and all and all that you care about."],[643.52,"Which is much less of a temptation for a monk or a nut."],[647.52,"They don't have that weighing on them."],[651.04,"But if any man thinks he is behaving improperly toward his virgin, if she is past the flower of youth and thus it must be, let him do what he wishes."],[659.68,"He does not sin, let them marry."],[662.24,"Okay, so this is again a weird phrase."],[667.28,"If any man thinks he's behaving improperly toward his virgin, sounds very strange."],[671.6,"This is talking about someone who's betrothed, someone who's engaged."],[674.68,"Okay, so a man is betrothed to this woman for whatever reason."],[679.4,"Maybe he doesn't have the money, maybe he doesn't, whatever it is."],[682.6,"For whatever reason, they haven't actually gotten married yet."],[685.98,"And this is what he's talking about."],[687.1,"If perhaps she is, I love the New King James, if perhaps she is beyond the flower of youth."],[692.8,"Meaning you're both getting older."],[695.78,"And you're not married yet."],[697.94,"And he says he does not sin, let them marry."],[700.27,"Go ahead and get married."],[701.57,"He's saying, I'm not saying that that it's wrong to get married in any way."],[709.73,"Nevertheless, he who stands steadfast in his heart, having no necessity, but has power over his own will, and is so determined in his heart. that he will keep his virgin does well"],[720.25,"So then he who gives her in marriage does well, but he who does not give her in marriage does better."],[725.45,"What is this?"],[726.89,"This. Now, this is talking about giving."],[731.23,"This is talking about a father with his daughter."],[734.19,"Saying a father with his daughter, a father giving his daughter to be married is not doing anything wrong."],[739.71,"But a father who doesn't is also not doing anything wrong."],[743.31,"They may be doing something better."],[750.47,"Paul, this is the beginning of the idea of consent."],[753.31,"The whole idea of consent that we so take for granted in the modern world, that's the basis of the whole MeToo movement, comes from St."],[761.23,"Paul and is Christian, as much as they would not want to hear that."],[762.11,"People who founded the Me Too movement"],[767.83,"This is the beginning of the idea of consent."],[770.31,"Consent begins with saints like Saint Thecla, who chose not to be married to the person they were betrothed to."],[780.07,"It begins with Saint Paul and the apostles saying that it's okay for her not to be married and to dedicate her life to following the Lord."],[793.11,"The model of that, of course, is the Theotokos."],[796.79,"But the teaching, because before anything was written about the Theotokos, Saint Paul was writing this letter."],[803.67,"The teaching that this was a good thing, is the beginning of that that idea a woman can say, you know what?"],[806.65,"I want to devote my life to following Christ"],[809.41,"I don't want to be married."],[814.21,"So a man who did that, again, might be showing, even the pagans will look at that and say, oh, what heroic sexual continence this person has."],[823.41,"But this is saying a woman can do that too."],[827.73,"A woman can do that too."],[829.17,"And so this is this is the beginning of that whole idea."],[832.87,"Is what St."],[833.51,"Paul has outlined in this chapter."],[835.59,"And the way he's he's applied sexual morality equally to both to both uh genders."],[844.47,"Yes, there's only two hate speech."],[849.67,"Careful with that."],[851.51,"This will be demonetized on YouTube"],[851.95,"I know. I know."],[853.23,"I think I'm safe on ancient faith."],[859.15,"So verse 39 a wife is bound by law as long as her husband lives, but if her husband dies, she is at liberty to be married to whom she wishes, only in the Lord."],[868.91,"But she is happier if she remains as she is, according to my judgment."],[872.83,"And I think I also have the Spirit of God."],[875.95,"So that last point is uh last point in this chapter, is again related."],[882.2,"A woman whose husband dies."],[884.36,"St. Paul is saying it's okay for her to remarry."],[888.44,"Be remarried. It's better if she doesn't, but it's okay if she does."],[892.26,"This is again situational."],[894.34,"The only caveat I put so there. is notice if she wants to be married, again, needs to be a Christian"],[900.7,"Saying if you're married to an unbeliever, you stay married, but if you're going to get married, you marry a fellow Christian."],[908.06,"So we'll go ahead and start chapter 8 here because it's short."],[912.62,"Chapter 8, verse 1."],[913.82,"Now, concerning things offered to idols, we know that we all have knowledge."],[918.38,"Knowledge puffs up, but love edifies."],[921.66,"So now he's shifting topics."],[923.98,"He's going to be talking about this for a while now, for a few chapters."],[927.26,"So he was talking about sexual morality, and now he is talking about, he's going to talk about food offered to idols."],[936.94,"Does anybody notice anything about those two topics?"],[939.66,"What they have in common?"],[941.3,"Those are two of the things from Acts 15 that the Gentiles were not allowed to do."],[946.9,"Remember, they talked about what parts of the Torah, what parts of the law apply to the Gentiles."],[952.66,"They had to refrain from sexual immorality."],[954.9,"They couldn't eat meat offered to idols."],[957.78,"Okay. So it's not coincidence that these are the things that St."],[963.22,"Paul is focusing on."],[965.22,"Sexual morality. Because these are the these are the these are the elements of greatest concern."],[971.54,"And of greatest concern in terms of being sins for the whole community."],[975.98,"are these These are basic principles that apply to Jews and Gentiles, sexual immorality and idolatry."],[982.02,"Now we're talking about things offered to idols."],[984.74,"There are several. cities"],[987.5,"Corinth is one of them, also Athens, Alexandria, where almost the entirety of the meat that you could buy in the meat market came from the pagan temples."],[999.18,"So the pagan temples are offering sacrifices sort of continuously."],[1004.3,"Most common thing sacrifice was actually pig."],[1006.78,"It's the most common sacrificial animal."],[1008.94,"That's because they breed fast and they grow fast."],[1013.28,"As opposed to like a cow."],[1014.98,"Or even a sheep."],[1016.58,"So uh they're also very easy to feed because they'll eat anything."],[1020.82,"So it's relatively easy to raise pigs, even in urban areas."],[1024.42,"But so there, but also other animals."],[1026.42,"And so this was going on continuously, daily at all the temples, they're sacrificing animals."],[1029.94,"And Greek sacrificial ritual was famous"],[1037.02,"Some of the Greek satirists actually make fun of it because they sort of noticed that the only parts that actually got offered to the god were the parts that nobody had any use for."],[1048.06,"So for example, Aristophanes referred to sacrifices as the burning of the bones because all the leather was taken to go and use by the leather merchants, and all of the meat went to the meat market, and everything they could sell at these other places, and then the bones, you know, would be left and the hooves and stuff, and they'd, you know, offer those to the god."],[1066.78,"Here you go, here's the stuff we don't want."],[1069.42,"But uh it wasn't only the meat market, the meat market. meat that came from these idols was actually left over from these feasts"],[1080.9,"So, for example, I mentioned in the introduction to 1 Corinthians the temple of Demeter there uh that's been excavated and had huge banquet halls, and we found the remains of all kinds of animals there, mostly pig."],[1095.32,"Because that was the most common, but all kinds of things, including exotic stuff like sea urchin remains and stuff."],[1101.96,"Now it's unclear what of that was sacrificed, and then there was also stuff that was called trapezomata in I don't know, technical nerd books that I read."],[1114.08,"Uh, which was other stuff that was on the it literally means table things in Greek."],[1118.2,"It's the other stuff that was served at the feast."],[1121.21,"So it may be, for example, that that uh a bull or pigs or what have you was what was sacrificed, and then these other foods were eaten with it at the banquet."],[1137.05,"Okay, but the sacrifices, remember, are not about killing things, because not all sacrifices involve killing anything, including Jewish sacrifices."],[1147.37,"A lot of them didn't involve killing any animals, but they all involve food because sacrifices were these shared meals, shared by the people, the priests, and the god, that bound the community together."],[1160.11,"And so that's why after the sacrifices, they had these feastss in the banquet hall where they all ate together."],[1163.79,"and Celebrating their unity with each other with the God of the city."],[1170.31,"With Demeter at the temple of Demeter."],[1172.71,"Uh, and then the leftover meat is taken and sold in the meat market."],[1177.35,"So this was one of the commandments in Acts 15 from the Council of Jerusalem."],[1183.91,"This is one of the things that even Gentiles could not could not do because eating that food was seen as partaking of taking part in the sacrifice, taking part in the sacrificial ritual, and binding yourself to that being part of that community which included this pagan god and included the pagans, in the city."],[1206.81,"So they're forbidden to do this."],[1207.77,"this. And idea goes goes back"],[1210.45,"We see this also like in the book of Daniel, when Daniel and his friends refused to eat the meat from the king's table, because the meat from the king's table all came from sacrifices."],[1220.45,"So they would only eat vegetables."],[1223.66,"There was that whole thing where they had the contest where they said, okay, well, we'll just eat our vegetables, and you eat the king's meat, we'll see who's healthier."],[1229.98,"And Daniel and his friends were healthier, and so they were allowed to not eat the king's meat."],[1234.78,"But um, so this this goes all the way right back to that."],[1239.18,"This is built into not just Jewish people's minds, but now through the Council of Jerusalem, the letter sent out by St."],[1246.3,"James and the other apostles."],[1248.38,"This is one of the basic commands."],[1250.38,"So this is the issue he's now turning. to"],[1253.38,"So keeping this commandment would mean that pretty much people living in the city of Corinth, people who weren't farmers raising their own food outside, wouldn't be able to eat meat if you don't eat meat offered."],[1266.34,"Because pretty much everything in their meat market was coming from the temples in Corinth."],[1271.06,"So he says, We know that we all have knowledge."],[1273.22,"Knowledge puffs up, but love edifies."],[1276.08,"So we all know things, we all have our opinions."],[1278.9,"So he's introducing this as I already know this is a topic."],[1282.26,"This is probably one of the things they asked about."],[1284.12,"I know this is the topic of some debate there."],[1286.92,"He's saying we all have our opinions."],[1288.86,"He says, but opinions, knowledge, this puffs up with pride."],[1292.86,"We're not about that, we're about love."],[1295.54,"Verse 2, if anyone thinks that he knows anything, he knows nothing as yet, nothing yet as he ought to know."],[1302.66,"But if anyone loves God, this one is known by him."],[1305.78,"So he says, if you think you know something, right now, remember, he already talked about in the beginning, he talked about knowledge."],[1313.15,"And how they were seeking after knowledge through the mysteries of Demeter and through these pagan rituals and this kind of thing."],[1318.35,"He's saying there's no knowledge to be had there."],[1321.33,"He says, so you guys need to not get hung up on what you think you know."],[1325.83,"Because even the things you know, you don't know the way you ought to know them."],[1329.17,"You don't really understand them the way you should."],[1331.91,"He said, but if you're someone who loves, then you're someone who God knows."],[1337.75,"And that's more important."],[1339.87,"Therefore, concerning the eating of things offered to idols, we know that an idol is nothing in the world, and that there is no other God but one."],[1347.23,"For even if there are so-called gods, whether in heaven or on earth, and there are many gods and many lords, yet for us there is one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we for him, and one Lord Jesus Christ, through whom are all things, and through whom we live."],[1363.79,"So he says, first."],[1366.39,"An idol is nothing in the world, and there's only one God."],[1370.07,"So he's saying, What's an idol?"],[1372.73,"He's he's he's actually yielding to some people a point, as we'll see here in a minute."],[1377.55,"It's a piece of wood, it's a rock."],[1379.71,"That's not a god."],[1380.83,"At least it's not a god like the god who we worship."],[1384.39,"Even if there are so-called gods, is a bad mistranslation."],[1393.03,"And it's a bad mistranslation with a purpose."],[1397.11,"Because in the 17th century, scholars invented the idea of monotheism."],[1402.87,"I know that may sound strange, but the term did not exist before the 17th century."],[1408.23,"And by monotheism, they meant people who believe that only one God exists."],[1414.63,"Okay? And the other ones don't exist."],[1418.31,"Now, St. Paul is going to contradict that in a couple of chapters."],[1422.47,"He's going to say all the gods of the nations are demons."],[1425.67,"He doesn't say they don't exist, he says they're demons, which is different."],[1430.41,"So, and in fact, right after they translate, they make St."],[1435.93,"Paul contradict himself."],[1437.39,"Even if there are so-called gods, and there are many gods and many lords."],[1441.47,"So what St."],[1442.19,"Paul actually says is for there are many things called gods."],[1445.23,"Many things that are called gods."],[1447.15,"Not so-called. He said, There are many things that are called gods that people call gods, that people worship."],[1453.48,"There's many gods and many lords out there that these people are worshiping."],[1456.76,"Just in Corinth, I'm sure there were dozens upon dozens, of shrines and temples and uh different gods."],[1465.68,"So there's all these beings out there that people worship."],[1470.04,"It says, but for us, there's one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we for him, and one Lord Jesus Christ, through whom are all things, and through whom we live."],[1482.18,"So there's only one for us."],[1484.16,"There's only one who we worship."],[1486.13,"So this is an important distinction."],[1488.05,"St. Paul doesn't believe that the Holy Trinity exists and all these other beings are fictional characters."],[1494.63,"And that's not what the Old Testament says."],[1496.31,"So that's why he doesn't believe it."],[1498.87,"When God says to Moses, Tonight I will judge the gods of Egypt."],[1502.85,"He's not saying tonight I'm going to judge a whole bunch of fictional characters."],[1506.13,"He's not saying I can beat up Superman."],[1508.29,"Okay. Yeah, he's a fictional character."],[1510.13,"Of course you can, me too."],[1511.67,"He doesn't exist."],[1512.39,"exist. He's talking about these beings that but aren't worthy of worship"],[1517.77,"There are these demonic beings who are being worshipped as gods, by the people and who hold them captive."],[1523.86,"So the other thing here that we can't really see in English, I mean notice there in verse 6, there's a lot of italicized words."],[1532.82,"Because there's a lot of English words that have been added."],[1535.94,"And the reason is they're trying to make sense of what Paul does in English."],[1539.22,"But what Paul is doing here is very sneaky."],[1541.94,"Because what St."],[1542.74,"Paul has done here is he's taken the most basic prayer of the Jewish world at the time, which is called the Shema."],[1552.02,"It's in Deuteronomy."],[1553.54,"It's called the Shema because that's the first word, which is the Hebrew word for hear or listen."],[1556.34,"is The Shema hear, O Israel, Yahweh your God, Yahweh is one."],[1567.4,"And that was prayed, Saint Paul uh would have prayed that in a lot of the same way that we as Orthodox Christians pray the Jesus prayer."],[1577.46,"Sort of the prayer of a single thought."],[1578.98,"That's how they did meditative prayer in the Jewish world."],[1581.94,"Is they would repeat that over and over to focus their mind and their attention on God."],[1587.86,"Now, in Greek, they most commonly transliterated, instead of putting the name Yahweh in, they would just put Kyrgios."],[1597.9,"They put Lore."],[1598.74,"That's why when we we read English Old Testaments, it says the Lord all the time instead of saying Yahweh."],[1605.44,"Because it's actually following what the Greek did in putting Lord there."],[1610.88,"Which means in Greek, it would read, Hear, O Israel, the Lord your God, the Lord is one."],[1618.08,"So what St."],[1618.96,"Paul did here in this verse in uh 1 Corinthians 8, verse 6, is he took all the words of the Shema in Greek and he stuck some in."],[1631.52,"So he added the Father."],[1634.64,"There's one God, the Father, of whom are all things that we've read, and one Lord Jesus Christ, through whom are all things, and through whom we live."],[1643.92,"So he's taken this statement."],[1645.12,"There's only one God"],[1646.28,"Here, O Israel, the Lord your God, the Lord is one."],[1651.4,"And he's inserted both the Father and Jesus Christ into it."],[1655.64,"There's many gods and many Lords."],[1657.16,"First, there's one God and one Lord."],[1659.48,"And by inserting them into the Shema, he's also saying that the two of them are one."],[1666.86,"Are one God."],[1667.9,"So this is one of the most direct ways."],[1671.82,"He says it a bunch of times, really."],[1673.74,"But this is one of the most direct ways that St."],[1676.78,"Paul is teaching that Christ is God."],[1680.46,"Christ is Yahweh."],[1681.9,"The Father is also, and that the two of them are one God."],[1687.5,"And he's saying this here because he's saying, okay, you're gonna say this idol."],[1694.69,"It's a rock, it's a piece of wood, it's nothing."],[1698.47,"And yeah, there's many gods and many lords out there, many things that are called gods, but for us, there's just the one God and the one lord."],[1705.61,"He's he's now citing the people who he said think they know something."],[1710.27,"This is what you're saying."],[1711.63,"This is what you're telling me."],[1714.35,"And so they're using this as their reason for why it's okay for them to eat this meat that was offered to idols."],[1722.59,"They're saying, look, I go to the meat market, I get some beef."],[1726.27,"I don't know for sure that it's been sacrificed, but what if it has?"],[1731.23,"So they went and waved it in front of a statue."],[1732.67,"They went and waved it in front of a big piece of wood"],[1734.2,"They went waved it in front of a rock."],[1738.76,"I only worship one God."],[1740.76,"I don't worship whatever that idol was supposed to represent."],[1744.98,"It's just meat."],[1746.18,"That's not anything."],[1747.22,"So why can't I just eat it?"],[1749.14,"That's their argument."],[1750.98,"Okay. That's their argument."],[1753.22,"So this is St."],[1754.1,"Paul's response. However, there is not in everyone that knowledge."],[1759.94,"For some with consciousness of the idol until now, eat it as a thing offered to an idol."],[1765.3,"And their conscience being weak is defiled."],[1768.02,"But food does not commend us to God, for neither if we eat are we the better, nor if we do not eat are we the worse."],[1774.98,"So he says. Look, okay, you say that"],[1781.02,"Right? He says, I'm not going to argue with you about that."],[1784.3,"But not everyone has that same understanding that you do."],[1789.1,"So you have people at your house and you serve this meat."],[1792.06,"Someone else who's eating there knows where it came from."],[1795.26,"Knows that this came from an idol."],[1797.58,"Knows that this was connected to this sacrifice, and he eats it."],[1802.06,"You've now caused him to violate his conscience."],[1806.86,"You've now disturbed and upset him."],[1809.58,"You've now led him into sin, basically."],[1812.62,"So remember, St."],[1813.5,"Paul's already talked about that."],[1815.36,"That if a person does something that they don't feel is right, they offend against their own conscience, that for them. is a sin"],[1822.74,"If they do it thinking me doing this is somehow defying God, or this is me somehow participating in this idol worship, and they do it anyway."],[1829.7,"They've sinned, regardless of what you may think about the objective."],[1835.92,"Whatever. And he says, look, you know, there's no what there's no virtue in you eating this when he says food does not command you to God."],[1845.0,"You eating this is not some display."],[1847.98,"Oh, I'm gonna eat this meat from an idol's temple, you know, and I don't care because that idol is nothing."],[1853.6,"Like this makes you a better Christian somehow."],[1856.5,"Because you have this knowledge and this understanding."],[1859.76,"So there's no benefit to you from eating that meatat. that you wouldn't get if you didn't e it"],[1865.48,"So you're eating it because you've decided it's okay, and you're causing this other person to offend against their conscience."],[1874.53,"And you've derived no benefit from it."],[1877.33,"So he says, but beware, let's somehow this liberty of yours because of becomes a stumbling block to those who are weak."],[1884.61,"For if anyone sees you who have knowledge eating in an idol's temple, will not the conscience of him who is weak be emboldened to eat those things offered to idols?"],[1895.81,"So he says, So, what happens?"],[1897.71,"So you participate in this festival in the city."],[1900.59,"You say, Look, I'm not worshiping the God."],[1902.99,"I'm just here eating some food with my neighbor, you know, socializing."],[1906.91,"Paul says, Okay, again, I'm not gonna argue with you"],[1909.59,"I'm not doing anything."],[1910.47,"St. But what happens when your fellow Christian sees you doing that?"],[1917.51,"He sees the people from the church are out celebrating at this pagan festival."],[1923.51,"Maybe that leads him to go and do it."],[1926.95,"Maybe he gets drawn back into the paganism."],[1929.83,"So again, is that worth what did you gain from participating?"],[1935.19,"Nothing. And you've hurt this other person."],[1937.69,"This is what he was talking about with knowledge and love."],[1940.11,"You're so puffed up with your knowledge."],[1941.71,"Well, what about love?"],[1943.23,"How about showing love to this other person?"],[1945.87,"And because of your knowledge shall the weak brother perish for whom Christ died."],[1950.35,"So he says, Look, this is someone who Christ died for."],[1952.71,"Christ loved him so much he died for them."],[1955.35,"Shouldn't you love them a little?"],[1957.13,"He's being kind of sarcastic."],[1958.7,"And you don't care at all."],[1959.9,"Because you just want to be able to eat your meat."],[1962.12,"You're just selfish."],[1963.88,"When you thus sin against the brethren and wound their weak conscience, you sin against Christ."],[1968.76,"Therefore, if food makes my brother stumble, I will never again eat meat unless I make my brother stumble."],[1974.52,"So St. Paul says, Hey, if I found out that me eating meat would cause one of these people to fall away, or to fall into sin, I'd never eat meat again the rest of my life."],[1983.1,"Now, St. Paul's going to return to this topic, because he doesn't really agree with this person with quote-unquote knowledge, which he sort of telegraphed by talking about how even the ones with knowledge don't know the way they think they know."],[1995.26,"He's going to return to this"],[1997.28,"But he's here giving one reason."],[1999.22,"He's saying, even if this is your opinion, you still shouldn't be eating it out of love for each other, which is more important than what you think you know."],[2008.1,"And so this is this is sort of an overarching principle for St."],[2013.86,"Paul, is that love sort of trumps over these other things."],[2019.06,"It trumps over quote unquote rights."],[2021.94,"And we as modern democratic people are very big about our rights."],[2026.34,"I have the right to do this and the right to do that, and how dare you."],[2030.66,"And what St."],[2031.86,"Paul is teaching, what Christianity teaches is the exact opposite."],[2034.67,"Is that we in humility have to always be willing to give up our own rights in order to show love to someone else and to help someone else."],[2041.75,"And that this is this is one of those commandments."],[2045.19,"This is part of that path, regardless of our calling, regardless of the way of life God has called us to in the place and the time when we live."],[2052.07,"This is one of these overarching principles."],[2065.51,"Time-wise, chapter one to wrap up."],[2068.79,"So thank you, everybody."],[2070.15,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2077.35,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancient faith.com."],[2082.71,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.88,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 7:20-24 - Slaves and free","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:20-24</b> remain in the calling in which you were called</li><li class=\"d1\">slave: don't be concerned; if you can be freed, <b>use it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">called as slave = Lord's <b>freedman</b>; called as free = <b>Christ's slave</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"bought at a price, <b>do not become slaves of men</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Still within <b>marriage</b>, giving related examples</li><li class=\"d0\">Christianity and slavery <b>not compatible</b> - but also not compatible with <b>disobedience or revolution</b></li><li class=\"d1\">slave convert: stay, <b>don't run away</b>, don't disobey civil commands</li><li class=\"d1\">owning another human being is <b>wrong</b>; \"bought with a price\" = by Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":107.13,"lv":3,"h":"Gregory of Nyssa on slaves","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"You shouldn't be owning each other\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Gregory of Nyssa</b>: Paschal orations, year after year → <b>free all your slaves</b> if Christian</li><li class=\"d1\">slavery <b>not yet outlawed</b> in 4th-c. Empire - later by Christians</li><li class=\"d1\">absurd to claim you own a Christian - <b>he's your brother</b></li></ul>"},{"t":149.71,"lv":3,"h":"Free are Christ's slaves; stay as you are","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Free: <b>Christ's slave</b> - He bought you, owns you</li><li class=\"d0\">Whatever state you became Christian in: <b>be content</b>, keep commandments, use gifts</li><li class=\"d0\">Christianity doesn't mean a different path</li><li class=\"d1\">don't leave spouse to be celibate; don't go find one</li><li class=\"d1\">don't become Jewish / stop being Jewish</li><li class=\"d1\">don't become free if slave / slave if free</li></ul>"},{"t":194.7,"lv":2,"h":"7:25-28 - Concerning virgins","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:25</b> \"no commandment from the Lord\" - <b>second time</b> he says this</li><li class=\"d1\">pastoral matters; nothing from Hebrew scriptures or Christ that answers it</li><li class=\"d1\">answering <b>their questions</b>; \"whom the Lord in his mercy has made <b>trustworthy</b>\" → best judgment</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:26-28</b> \"present distress\": good to <b>remain as you are</b></li><li class=\"d1\">bound to a wife - don't seek to be loosed; loose - don't seek a wife</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>marry and you have not sinned</b>; \"trouble in the flesh\", I would spare you</li><li class=\"d0\">Doesn't say okay to <b>divorce</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Distress = problems at Corinth, some already heard, more coming</li></ul>"},{"t":318.28,"lv":2,"h":"7:29-31 - The time is short","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:29-31</b> those with wives as though none; weep as not weeping; buy as not possessing; use world as <b>not misusing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"form of this world is <b>passing away</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not (only) \"Paul thought Jesus returning <b>right now</b>\" - could return then, as now</li><li class=\"d0\">Point: we're <b>deeply involved in this world</b></li><li class=\"d1\">single: finding spouse; career, wealth, slave or free, what we eat</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not the <b>ultimate things</b>\" - we'll <b>die</b> or Christ will <b>return</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Mourning and rejoicing: <b>detach</b> a little</li><li class=\"d1\">this world's concerns pull attention from <b>Christ</b> - part of Corinth's problem</li><li class=\"d0\">Their list of questions = day-to-day concerns, important but not <b>ultimate</b></li></ul>"},{"t":497.31,"lv":2,"h":"7:32-35 - Without care","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:32-35</b> \"I want you without care\"</li><li class=\"d1\">unmarried cares for <b>things of the Lord</b>; married for things of the world, <b>pleasing wife</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same for unmarried woman / married woman</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not to put a <b>leash</b> on you\" - serve the Lord <b>without distraction</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why celibacy: more <b>detached</b>, focus on ultimate concern</li><li class=\"d0\">= <b>foundation of monasticism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">celibate, owning no property → dedicated to the one concern</li></ul>"},{"t":578.04,"lv":3,"h":"The married person's focus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Married: <b>right</b> to care for family - not providing for kids would be a problem</li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>multiple goals</b> in mind</li><li class=\"d1\">more <b>temptation</b> to focus on this world</li><li class=\"d0\">\"as though unmarried\" = stay focused on ultimate concern</li><li class=\"d1\">not consumed by <b>paying bills</b> and \"new shoes because the kids outgrew them for the 18th time\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Much less a temptation for a <b>monk or a nun</b></li></ul>"},{"t":651.04,"lv":2,"h":"7:36-38 - The betrothed virgin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:36</b> man behaving \"improperly toward his virgin\" = <b>betrothed, engaged</b></li><li class=\"d1\">haven't married - no money, whatever reason</li><li class=\"d1\">\"beyond the flower of youth\" = both getting older</li><li class=\"d1\">he does not sin - <b>let them marry</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:37</b> steadfast, no necessity, power over his own will, <b>keeps his virgin</b> = does well</li></ul>"},{"t":731.23,"lv":3,"h":"7:38 - A father and his daughter; consent","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:38</b> giving her in marriage does well; not giving does <b>better</b></li><li class=\"d1\">father with his daughter - <b>neither wrong</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Beginning of the idea of <b>consent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">basis of the \"MeToo movement\" comes from Paul, <b>Christian</b></li><li class=\"d1\">starts with saints like <b>St Thecla</b>, who chose not to marry her betrothed</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul and apostles: okay for her to <b>dedicate her life to the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d1\">model = the <b>Theotokos</b> - but Paul wrote before anything written about her</li><li class=\"d0\">Pagans admired <b>male</b> continence; Paul: <b>a woman can too</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sexual morality applied <b>equally to both genders</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: \"hate speech\", \"demonetized on YouTube\" - \"safe on Ancient Faith\"</li></ul>"},{"t":859.15,"lv":2,"h":"7:39-40 - A widow may remarry in the Lord","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:39-40</b> wife bound while husband lives; if he dies, free to marry <b>only in the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d1\">happier if she remains - \"my judgment\"; \"I also have the <b>Spirit of God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Okay to remarry; <b>better not</b> - situational</li><li class=\"d0\">Caveat: marry a <b>fellow Christian</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if married to an unbeliever, stay married</li></ul>"},{"t":908.06,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 8:1-3 - Food offered to idols","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Start <b>chapter 8</b> because short</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:1</b> \"we all have knowledge. Knowledge <b>puffs up</b>, but <b>love edifies</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">New topic for a few chapters: <b>food offered to idols</b></li><li class=\"d1\">after sexual morality - both are in <b>Acts 15</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Gentiles to refrain from sexual immorality and <b>meat offered to idols</b></li><li class=\"d1\">basic principles for Jews and Gentiles: <b>sexual immorality and idolatry</b></li></ul>"},{"t":984.74,"lv":3,"h":"The meat market and the temples","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Corinth, <b>Athens</b>, <b>Alexandria</b>: nearly all market meat came from pagan temples</li><li class=\"d0\">Daily sacrifice; most common = <b>pig</b> - breed fast, grow fast, eat anything</li><li class=\"d0\">Greek satirists: only parts nobody wanted went to the god</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Aristophanes</b>: \"burning of the bones\" - leather to merchants, meat to market</li><li class=\"d0\">Leftovers from <b>feasts</b> too</li><li class=\"d1\">temple of <b>Demeter</b> excavated: huge banquet halls, mostly pig, <b>sea urchin</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>trapezomata</i> = \"table things\", other food at the feast</li></ul>"},{"t":1137.05,"lv":3,"h":"Sacrifice as shared meal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Sacrifices not about <b>killing</b> - many involved no animals, incl. Jewish</li><li class=\"d0\">All involve <b>food</b>: meal shared by people, priests, god → binds the community</li><li class=\"d0\">Banquet after, celebrating unity with the <b>god of the city</b>; leftovers sold</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts 15</b> forbade it - eating = <b>taking part</b> in the sacrifice and the community</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Daniel</b> refusing the king's table - vegetables, healthier</li><li class=\"d0\">Built into Jewish minds and via <b>Council of Jerusalem</b> / St James's letter</li><li class=\"d0\">→ in Corinth, non-farmers couldn't eat meat at all</li></ul>"},{"t":1295.54,"lv":2,"h":"8:2-3 - Knowledge versus love","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:1</b> we all have opinions; knowledge <b>puffs up</b>; \"we're about <b>love</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">probably something they asked about - a topic of debate</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:2-3</b> if anyone thinks he knows, knows nothing <b>as he ought</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they sought knowledge in <b>Demeter's mysteries</b> - none to be had</li><li class=\"d1\">loves God → <b>known by Him</b> - more important</li></ul>"},{"t":1339.87,"lv":2,"h":"8:4-6 - One God, many so-called gods","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:4</b> idol is nothing; no other God but one</li><li class=\"d1\">piece of wood, a rock - not a god like ours</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:5-6</b> \"so-called gods\" = <b>bad mistranslation</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>monotheism</b> invented 17th c. - only one God exists, others don't</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul later: gods of nations are <b>demons</b> - exist, not fictional</li><li class=\"d1\">actual: many things <b>called</b> gods, many gods and lords people worship</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>One God the Father</b> and <b>one Lord Jesus Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God to Moses: \"tonight I judge the <b>gods of Egypt</b>\" - not \"beating up Superman\"</li><li class=\"d1\">demonic beings worshipped as gods, holding people captive</li></ul>"},{"t":1539.22,"lv":3,"h":"The Shema in 8:6","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Italicized words in English - Paul doing something sneaky</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Shema</b> (Deut. 6) - Hebrew \"hear\": \"Hear O Israel, Yahweh your God, Yahweh is one\"</li><li class=\"d1\">prayed like the <b>Jesus Prayer</b> - prayer of a single thought, repeated</li><li class=\"d0\">Greek: <i>Kyrios</i> for Yahweh → \"the Lord your God, the Lord is one\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>inserts</b> the Father and Jesus Christ into the Shema</li><li class=\"d1\">\"one God, the Father... one Lord Jesus Christ\" → the two are <b>one God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">One of most direct ways: <b>Christ is Yahweh</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1687.5,"lv":3,"h":"The knowers' argument","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul now citing those who \"think they know\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"idol is a rock, nothing - there are many gods out there, <b>but for us one</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I get beef at the market; maybe waved before a statue - <b>just meat</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Their reason it's okay to eat</li></ul>"},{"t":1754.1,"lv":2,"h":"8:7-8 - The weak conscience","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:7</b> not everyone has that knowledge; some eat as offered to an idol → conscience <b>defiled</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul not arguing the point, but others differ</li><li class=\"d1\">you serve it at home; one who knows origin eats → <b>violates conscience</b>, led into sin</li><li class=\"d0\">Violating conscience = <b>sin</b> for them, whatever the objective truth</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:8</b> food doesn't commend us to God - no virtue, no benefit</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't make you a better Christian</li></ul>"},{"t":1877.33,"lv":2,"h":"8:9-12 - A stumbling block","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:9-10</b> your liberty a <b>stumbling block</b> to the weak</li><li class=\"d1\">weak brother sees you eating in an <b>idol's temple</b> → emboldened</li><li class=\"d1\">festival, \"just socializing\" - fellow Christian sees church people at pagan festival → drawn back into <b>paganism</b></li><li class=\"d0\">What did you gain? <b>Nothing</b> - and you've hurt him</li><li class=\"d0\">Knowledge vs <b>love</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:11</b> weak brother perishes, <b>for whom Christ died</b> - sarcastic</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you're just selfish\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:12</b> wound conscience → <b>sin against Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1968.76,"lv":3,"h":"8:13 - Love over rights","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>8:13</b> if food makes brother stumble, <b>never again eat meat</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul will <b>return to this</b> - doesn't agree with the \"knowledge\" person</li><li class=\"d1\">even if your opinion's right, don't eat out of <b>love</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Overarching principle: <b>love trumps rights</b></li><li class=\"d1\">modern democratic people: \"I have the <b>right</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christianity opposite: in <b>humility</b> give up rights for another</li><li class=\"d0\">Part of the path regardless of calling, way of life, time and place</li></ul>"}]});
